1
00:00:00,580 --> 00:00:03,759
part 3 chapter 2.

2
00:00:03,759 --> 00:00:07,957
He was lying on something that felt like a camp bed, except that it was higher off

3
00:00:07,957 --> 00:00:11,680
the ground and that he was fixed down in some way so that he could not move

4
00:00:11,680 --> 00:00:15,700
lights that seemed stronger than usual was falling on his face.

5
00:00:15,700 --> 00:00:19,540
O'Brien was standing at his side, looking down at him intently.

6
00:00:19,540 --> 00:00:24,580
At the other side of him stood a man in a white coat, holding a hypodermic syringe.

7
00:00:24,580 --> 00:00:29,378
Even after his eyes were open he took in his surroundings only gradually.

8
00:00:29,378 --> 00:00:32,860
He had the impression of swimming up into this room from some quite different

9
00:00:32,860 --> 00:00:36,520
world, a sort of underwater world far beneath it.

10
00:00:36,520 --> 00:00:39,400
How long he had been down there he did not know.

11
00:00:39,400 --> 00:00:43,659
Since the moment when they arrested him he had not seen darkness or daylight.

12
00:00:43,659 --> 00:00:46,419
Besides, his memories were not continuous.

13
00:00:46,419 --> 00:00:50,080
There had been times when consciousness, even the sort of consciousness that one

14
00:00:50,080 --> 00:00:54,700
has in sleep, had stopped dead and started again after a blank interval.

15
00:00:54,700 --> 00:01:00,040
But whether the intervals were of days or weeks or only seconds, there was no way of knowing.

16
00:01:00,040 --> 00:01:03,700
With that first blow on the elbow the nightmare had started.

17
00:01:03,700 --> 00:01:08,259
Later he was to realize that all that then happened was merely a preliminary, a

18
00:01:08,259 --> 00:01:11,919
routine interrogation to which nearly all prisoners were subjected.

19
00:01:11,919 --> 00:01:16,900
There was a long range of crimes— —espionage, sabotage and the like— —to which

20
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:19,419
everyone had to confess as a matter of course.

21
00:01:19,419 --> 00:01:23,500
The confession was a formality, though the torture was real.

22
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:28,540
How many times he had been beaten, how long the beatings had continued, he could not remember.

23
00:01:28,540 --> 00:01:32,977
Always there were five or six men in black uniforms at him simultaneously.

24
00:01:32,977 --> 00:01:37,477
Sometimes it was fists, sometimes it was truncheons, sometimes it was steel rods,

25
00:01:37,477 --> 00:01:39,878
sometimes it was boots.

26
00:01:39,878 --> 00:01:43,058
There were times when he rolled about the floor, as shameless as an animal,

27
00:01:43,058 --> 00:01:47,080
writhing his body this way into that in an endless, hopeless effort to dodge the

28
00:01:47,080 --> 00:01:52,180
kicks, and simply inviting more and yet more kicks, in his ribs, in his belly, on

29
00:01:52,180 --> 00:01:56,860
his elbows, on his shins, in his groin, in his testicles, on the bone at the base of

30
00:01:56,860 --> 00:01:58,240
his spine.

31
00:01:58,240 --> 00:02:01,718
There were times when it went on and on until the cruel, wicked, unforgivable

32
00:02:01,718 --> 00:02:05,740
thing seemed to him not that the gods continued to beat him but that he could

33
00:02:05,740 --> 00:02:08,558
not force himself into losing consciousness.

34
00:02:08,558 --> 00:02:11,977
There were times when his nerve so forsook him that he began shouting for

35
00:02:11,977 --> 00:02:16,058
mercy even before the beating began, when the mere sight of a fist drawn back for

36
00:02:16,058 --> 00:02:21,159
a blow was enough to make him pour forth a confession of real and imaginary crimes.

37
00:02:21,159 --> 00:02:25,360
There were other times when he started out with the resolve of confessing nothing,

38
00:02:25,360 --> 00:02:29,860
when every word had to be forced out of him between gasps of pain, and there were

39
00:02:29,860 --> 00:02:33,580
times when he feebly tried to compromise, when he said to himself:

40
00:02:33,580 --> 00:02:37,659
'I will confess, but not yet. I must hold out till the pain becomes unbearable.

41
00:02:37,659 --> 00:02:42,637
Three more kicks, two more kicks, and then I will tell them what they want.'

42
00:02:42,637 --> 00:02:46,840
Sometimes he was beaten till he could hardly stand, then flung like a sack of

43
00:02:46,840 --> 00:02:51,279
potatoes onto the stone floor of a cell, left to recuperate for a few hours, and

44
00:02:51,279 --> 00:02:53,439
then taken out and beaten again.

45
00:02:53,439 --> 00:02:56,439
There were also longer periods of recovery.

46
00:02:56,439 --> 00:03:01,240
He remembered the dimly, because they were spent chiefly in sleep or stupa

47
00:03:01,240 --> 00:03:05,080
He remembered a cell with a plank bed, a sort of shelf sticking out from the wall,

48
00:03:05,080 --> 00:03:10,119
and a tin wash basin and meals of hot soup and bread and sometimes coffee.

49
00:03:10,119 --> 00:03:14,319
He remembered a surly barber arriving to scrape his chin and crop his hair, and a

50
00:03:14,319 --> 00:03:18,457
business-like unsympathetic men in white coats feeling his pulse, tapping his

51
00:03:18,457 --> 00:03:22,779
reflexes, turning up his eyelids, running harsh fingers over him in search for

52
00:03:22,779 --> 00:03:27,040
broken bones, and shooting needles into his arm to make him sleep.

53
00:03:27,040 --> 00:03:31,718
The beatings grew less frequent, and became mainly a threat, a horror to which

54
00:03:31,718 --> 00:03:35,500
he could be sent back at any moment when his answers were unsatisfactory.

55
00:03:35,500 --> 00:03:39,637
His questioners now were not ruffians in black uniforms but Party intellectuals,

56
00:03:39,637 --> 00:03:44,020
little rotunda men with quick movements and flashing spectacles, who worked on

57
00:03:44,020 --> 00:03:48,637
him in relays over periods which lasted— —he thought, he could not be sure 10 or 12

58
00:03:48,637 --> 00:03:50,319
hours at a stretch.

59
00:03:50,319 --> 00:03:54,457
These other questioners saw to it that the team was in constant slight pain, but

60
00:03:54,457 --> 00:03:57,099
it was not chiefly pain that they relied on.

61
00:03:57,099 --> 00:04:01,297
They slapped his face rung his ears, pulled his hair, made him stand on one

62
00:04:01,297 --> 00:04:06,099
leg, refused him leave to urinate strong glaring lights in his face until his

63
00:04:06,099 --> 00:04:10,540
eyes ran with water; but the aim of this was simply to humiliate him and destroy

64
00:04:10,540 --> 00:04:13,000
his power of arguing and reasoning.

65
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:16,659
Their real weapon was the merciless questioning that went on and on, hour

66
00:04:16,659 --> 00:04:21,058
after hour, tripping him up, laying traps for him, twisting everything that he said,

67
00:04:21,058 --> 00:04:25,180
convicting him at every step of lies and self-contradiction until he began

68
00:04:25,180 --> 00:04:28,619
weeping as much from shame as from nervous fatigue.

69
00:04:28,619 --> 00:04:32,378
Sometimes he would reap half a dozen times in a single session.

70
00:04:32,378 --> 00:04:35,919
Most of the time they screamed abuse at him and threatened at every hesitation

71
00:04:35,919 --> 00:04:40,297
to deliver him over to the guards again; but sometimes they would suddenly change

72
00:04:40,297 --> 00:04:44,200
their tune, call him comrade, appeal to him in the name of insock and Big

73
00:04:44,200 --> 00:04:48,520
Brother, and ask him sorrowfully whether even now he had not enough loyalty to

74
00:04:48,520 --> 00:04:52,360
the Party left to make him wish to undo the evil he had done.

75
00:04:52,360 --> 00:04:56,680
When his nerves were in rags after hours of questioning, even this appeal could

76
00:04:56,680 --> 00:04:58,779
reduce him to sniveling tears.

77
00:04:58,779 --> 00:05:02,439
In the end of the nagging voices broke him down more completely than the boots

78
00:05:02,439 --> 00:05:07,297
and fists of the guards. He became simply a mouse that uttered, a hand that signed,

79
00:05:07,297 --> 00:05:09,340
whatever was demanded of him.

80
00:05:09,340 --> 00:05:13,000
His sole concern was to find out what they wanted him to confess, and then

81
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:17,500
confess it quickly, before the bullying started anew. He confessed to the

82
00:05:17,500 --> 00:05:21,878
assassination of eminent Party members, the distribution of seditious pamphlets,

83
00:05:21,878 --> 00:05:27,457
embezzlement of public funds, sale of military secrets, sabotage of every kind.

84
00:05:27,457 --> 00:05:31,180
He confessed that he had been a spy in the pay of the East Asian government as

85
00:05:31,180 --> 00:05:33,340
far back as 1968..

86
00:05:33,340 --> 00:05:37,540
He confessed that he was a religious believer and admirer of capitalism and a

87
00:05:37,540 --> 00:05:41,919
sexual pervert. He confessed that he had murdered his wife, although he knew, and

88
00:05:41,919 --> 00:05:45,218
his questioners must have known, that his wife was still alive.

89
00:05:45,218 --> 00:05:49,540
He confessed that for years he had been in personal touch with Goldstone and had

90
00:05:49,540 --> 00:05:52,540
been a member of an underground organization which had included almost

91
00:05:52,540 --> 00:05:56,439
every human being he had ever known. It was easier to confess everything and

92
00:05:56,439 --> 00:05:58,180
implicate everybody.

93
00:05:58,180 --> 00:06:02,558
Besides, in a sense it was all true. It was true that he had been the enemy of

94
00:06:02,558 --> 00:06:05,860
the Party, and in the eyes of the Party there was no distinction between the

95
00:06:05,860 --> 00:06:09,580
thought and the deed. There were also memories of another kind.

96
00:06:09,580 --> 00:06:14,919
They stood out in his mind disconnected he liked pictures with blackness all around them.

97
00:06:14,919 --> 00:06:18,457
He was in a self which might have been either dark or light, because he could

98
00:06:18,457 --> 00:06:20,619
see nothing except a pair of eyes.

99
00:06:20,619 --> 00:06:24,700
Near at hand some kind of instrument was ticking slowly and regularly.

100
00:06:24,700 --> 00:06:27,340
The eyes grew larger and more luminous.

101
00:06:27,340 --> 00:06:33,000
Suddenly he floated out of his seat, dived into the eyes and was swallowed up.

102
00:06:33,040 --> 00:06:37,297
He was strapped into a chair surrounded by dials, under dazzling lights. A man in

103
00:06:37,297 --> 00:06:41,797
a white coat was reading the dials. There was a trap of heavy boots outside. The

104
00:06:41,797 --> 00:06:46,957
door clanged The wax faced officer marched in, followed by two guards.

105
00:06:46,957 --> 00:06:50,020
'Room 101,' said the officer.

106
00:06:50,020 --> 00:06:53,860
The man in the white coat did not turn around He did not look at Winston either;

107
00:06:53,860 --> 00:06:56,558
he was looking only at the dials.

108
00:06:56,558 --> 00:07:00,700
He was rolling down a mighty corridor, a kilometer wide, full of glorious, golden

109
00:07:00,700 --> 00:07:05,080
light, roaring with laughter and shouting out confessions at the top of his voice.

110
00:07:05,080 --> 00:07:08,680
He was confessing everything, even the things he had succeeded in holding back

111
00:07:08,680 --> 00:07:10,119
under the torture.

112
00:07:10,119 --> 00:07:14,439
He was relating the entire history of his life to an audience who knew it already.

113
00:07:14,439 --> 00:07:17,919
With him with the gods the other question is the men in white coats,

114
00:07:17,919 --> 00:07:22,599
O'Brien, Julia, Mr Charrington, all rolling down the corridor together and shouting

115
00:07:22,599 --> 00:07:26,500
with laughter. Some dreadful thing which had land embedded in the future had

116
00:07:26,500 --> 00:07:29,259
somehow been skipped over and had not happened.

117
00:07:29,259 --> 00:07:32,977
Everything was all right, there was no more pain, the last detail of his life

118
00:07:32,977 --> 00:07:36,279
was laid bare, understood, forgiven.

119
00:07:36,279 --> 00:07:41,259
He was starting up from the plank bed in the half certainty that he had heard O'Brien's voice.

120
00:07:41,259 --> 00:07:44,919
All through his interrogation, although he had never seen him, he had had the

121
00:07:44,919 --> 00:07:48,279
feeling that O'Brien was at his elbow, just out of sight.

122
00:07:48,279 --> 00:07:52,058
It was O'Brien who was directing everything. It was he who set the guards

123
00:07:52,058 --> 00:07:54,819
onto Winston and who prevented them from killing him.

124
00:07:54,819 --> 00:07:58,599
It was he who decided when Winston should scream with pain, when he should

125
00:07:58,599 --> 00:08:02,740
have a respite, when he should be fed, when he should sleep, when the drugs

126
00:08:02,740 --> 00:08:04,659
should be pumped into his arm.

127
00:08:04,659 --> 00:08:08,319
It was he who asked the questions and suggested the answers.

128
00:08:08,319 --> 00:08:13,419
He was the tormentor, he was the protector, he was the inquisitor, he was the friend.

129
00:08:13,419 --> 00:08:17,020
And once— —Winston could not remember whether it was in drug to sleep, or in

130
00:08:17,020 --> 00:08:22,058
normal sleep, or even in a moment of wakefulness— —a voice murmured in his ear:

131
00:08:22,058 --> 00:08:24,580
'Don't worry, Winston you're in my keeping.

132
00:08:24,580 --> 00:08:27,099
For seven years I have watched over you.

133
00:08:27,099 --> 00:08:32,439
Now the Turning Point has come. I shall save you, I shall make you perfect.'

134
00:08:32,439 --> 00:08:36,520
He was not sure whether it was O'Brien's voice; but it was the same voice that had

135
00:08:36,520 --> 00:08:40,297
said to him, 'We shall meet in the place where there is no darkness,' in that other

136
00:08:40,297 --> 00:08:42,700
dream, seven years ago.

137
00:08:42,700 --> 00:08:46,180
He did not remember any ending to his interrogation.

138
00:08:46,180 --> 00:08:50,378
There was a period of blackness and then the cell, or room, in which he now was had

139
00:08:50,378 --> 00:08:52,840
gradually materialized around him.

140
00:08:52,840 --> 00:08:56,259
He was almost flat on his back, and unable to move.

141
00:08:56,259 --> 00:08:59,977
His body was held down at every essential point. Even the back of his

142
00:08:59,977 --> 00:09:02,020
head was gripped in some manner.

143
00:09:02,020 --> 00:09:05,439
.O'Brien was looking down at him gravely and rather sadly.

144
00:09:05,439 --> 00:09:10,000
His face, seen from below, looked coarse and worn, with pouches under the eyes and

145
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:12,457
tired lines from nose to chin.

146
00:09:12,457 --> 00:09:17,860
He was older than Winston had thought him; he was perhaps 48 or 50. Under his

147
00:09:17,860 --> 00:09:22,900
hand there was a dial with a lever on top and figures running around the face.

148
00:09:22,900 --> 00:09:27,400
'I told you,' said O'Brien, 'that if we met again it would be here.'

149
00:09:27,400 --> 00:09:30,218
'Yes,' said Winston.

150
00:09:30,218 --> 00:09:36,159
Without any warning except a slight movement of O'Brien's hand, a wave of pain flooded his body.

151
00:09:36,159 --> 00:09:39,878
It was a frightening pain, because he could not see what was happening, and he

152
00:09:39,878 --> 00:09:43,119
had the feeling that some mortal injury was being done to him.

153
00:09:43,119 --> 00:09:46,599
He did not know whether the thing was really happening, or whether the effect

154
00:09:46,599 --> 00:09:50,977
was electrically produced; but his body was being wrenched out of shape, the

155
00:09:50,977 --> 00:09:53,378
joints were being slowly torn apart.

156
00:09:53,378 --> 00:09:56,977
Although the pain had brought the sweat out on his forehead, the worst of all was

157
00:09:56,977 --> 00:09:59,558
the fear that his backbone was about to snap.

158
00:09:59,558 --> 00:10:05,797
He set his teeth and breathed hard through his nose, trying to keep silent as long as possible.

159
00:10:05,797 --> 00:10:10,119
'You are afraid,' said O'Brien, watching his face, 'that in another moment something is

160
00:10:10,119 --> 00:10:11,200
going to break.

161
00:10:11,200 --> 00:10:15,520
Your special fear is that it will be your backbone. You have a vivid mental

162
00:10:15,520 --> 00:10:19,659
picture of the vertebrae snapping apart and the spinal fluid dripping out of them.

163
00:10:19,659 --> 00:10:22,957
That is what you're thinking, is it not, Winston?'

164
00:10:22,957 --> 00:10:24,819
Winston did not answer.

165
00:10:24,819 --> 00:10:27,520
O'Brien drew back the lever on the dial.

166
00:10:27,520 --> 00:10:32,340
The wave of pain receded almost as quickly as it had come.

167
00:10:32,558 --> 00:10:36,580
'That was 40 said O'Brien. 'You can see that the numbers on this dial run up to

168
00:10:36,580 --> 00:10:37,718
a hundred.

169
00:10:37,718 --> 00:10:41,378
Will you please remember, throughout our conversation, that I have it in my power

170
00:10:41,378 --> 00:10:45,819
to inflict pain on you at any moment and to whatever degree I choose.

171
00:10:45,819 --> 00:10:50,797
If you tell me any lies, or attempt to prevaricate in any way, or even fall

172
00:10:50,797 --> 00:10:55,240
below your usual level of intelligence, you will cry out with pain, instantly. Do

173
00:10:55,240 --> 00:10:56,740
you understand that?'

174
00:10:56,740 --> 00:10:59,020
'Yes,' said Winston.

175
00:10:59,020 --> 00:11:03,457
O'Brien's manner became less severe. He resettled his spectacles thoughtfully,

176
00:11:03,457 --> 00:11:06,279
and took a pace or two up and down.

177
00:11:06,279 --> 00:11:10,477
When he spoke his voice was gentle and patient. He had the heir of a doctor, a

178
00:11:10,477 --> 00:11:16,477
teacher, even a priest, anxious to explain and persuade rather than to punish.

179
00:11:16,477 --> 00:11:20,500
'I am taking trouble with you, Winston,' he said, 'because you are worth trouble.

180
00:11:20,500 --> 00:11:24,159
You know perfectly well what is the matter with you. You have known it for

181
00:11:24,159 --> 00:11:26,619
years, though you have fought against the knowledge.

182
00:11:26,619 --> 00:11:31,000
You were mentally deranged. You suffer from a defective memory. You are unable

183
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:34,718
to remember real events, and you persuade yourself that you remember other events

184
00:11:34,718 --> 00:11:36,159
which never happened.

185
00:11:36,159 --> 00:11:41,378
Fortunately it is curable. You have never cured yourself of it, because you did not choose to.

186
00:11:41,378 --> 00:11:44,619
There was a small effort of the will that you were not ready to make.

187
00:11:44,619 --> 00:11:47,797
Even now, I am well aware, you are clinging to your disease under the

188
00:11:47,797 --> 00:11:50,020
impression that it is a virtue.

189
00:11:50,020 --> 00:11:56,797
Now we will take an example. At this moment, which power is Oceania at war with?'

190
00:11:56,797 --> 00:12:02,977
'When I was arrested, Oceania was at war with East Asia With East Asia Good.

191
00:12:02,977 --> 00:12:07,058
And Oceania has always been at war with East Asia has it not?'

192
00:12:07,058 --> 00:12:11,977
Winston drew in his breath. He opened his mouth to speak and then did not speak. He

193
00:12:11,977 --> 00:12:14,619
could not take his eyes away from the dial.

194
00:12:14,619 --> 00:12:21,159
'The truth, please, Winston. Your truth. Tell me what you think you remember.'

195
00:12:21,159 --> 00:12:25,058
'I remember that until only a week before I was arrested, we were not at war with

196
00:12:25,058 --> 00:12:29,740
East Asia at all. We were in alliance with them. The war was against Eurasia.

197
00:12:29,740 --> 00:12:35,080
That had lasted for four years. Before that that Brian stopped him with the movement

198
00:12:35,080 --> 00:12:36,700
of the hand.

199
00:12:36,700 --> 00:12:38,797
'Another example,' he said.

200
00:12:38,797 --> 00:12:42,279
'Some years ago you had a very serious delusion indeed.

201
00:12:42,279 --> 00:12:45,878
You believed that three men, three one-time Party members named Jones,

202
00:12:45,878 --> 00:12:49,659
Aaronson and Rutherford— —men who were executed for treachery and sabotage

203
00:12:49,659 --> 00:12:53,797
after making the fullest possible confession— —were not guilty of the crimes they were

204
00:12:53,797 --> 00:12:54,878
charged with.

205
00:12:54,878 --> 00:12:58,119
You believed that you had seen unmistakable documentary evidence

206
00:12:58,119 --> 00:13:00,759
proving that their confessions were false.

207
00:13:00,759 --> 00:13:04,119
There was a certain photograph about which you had a hallucination.

208
00:13:04,119 --> 00:13:11,137
You believe that you actually held it in your hands. It was a photograph something like this.'

209
00:13:11,137 --> 00:13:15,457
An oblong slip of newspaper had appeared between O'Brien's fingers. For perhaps

210
00:13:15,457 --> 00:13:18,878
five seconds it was within the angle of Winston's vision.

211
00:13:18,878 --> 00:13:22,599
It was a photograph, and there was no question of its identity.

212
00:13:22,599 --> 00:13:24,279
It was the photograph.

213
00:13:24,279 --> 00:13:28,058
It was another copy of the photograph of Jones, Aaronson and Rutherford at the

214
00:13:28,058 --> 00:13:33,340
Party function in New York, which he had chanced upon 11 years ago and promptly destroyed.

215
00:13:33,340 --> 00:13:38,200
For only an instant it was before his eyes, then it was out of sight again. But

216
00:13:38,200 --> 00:13:43,058
he had seen it, unquestionably he had seen it! He made a desperate agonizing

217
00:13:43,058 --> 00:13:47,080
effort to wrench the top half of his body free. It was impossible to move so

218
00:13:47,080 --> 00:13:49,419
much as a centimeter in any direction.

219
00:13:49,419 --> 00:13:52,119
For the moment he had even forgotten the dial.

220
00:13:52,119 --> 00:13:57,520
All he wanted was to hold the photograph in his fingers again, or at least to see it.

221
00:13:57,520 --> 00:13:59,619
'It exists!' he cried.

222
00:13:59,619 --> 00:14:01,659
'No,' said O'Brien.

223
00:14:01,659 --> 00:14:06,218
He stepped across the room. There was a memory hole in the opposite wall. O'Brien

224
00:14:06,218 --> 00:14:10,419
lifted the grating. Unseen, the frail slip of paper was whirling away on the

225
00:14:10,419 --> 00:14:14,439
current of warm air; it was vanishing in a flash of flame.

226
00:14:14,439 --> 00:14:17,259
O'Brien turned away from the wall.

227
00:14:17,259 --> 00:14:22,419
'Ashes,' he said. 'Not even identifiable ashes. Dust. It does not exist.

228
00:14:22,419 --> 00:14:24,700
It never existed.'

229
00:14:24,700 --> 00:14:32,500
'But it did exist! It does exist! It exists in memory. I remember it. You remember it.'

230
00:14:32,500 --> 00:14:35,259
'I do not remember it,' said O'Brien.

231
00:14:35,259 --> 00:14:41,200
Winston's heart sank. That was double think He had a feeling of deadly helplessness.

232
00:14:41,200 --> 00:14:44,439
If you could have been certain that O'Brien was lying, it would not have

233
00:14:44,439 --> 00:14:47,977
seemed to matter. But it was perfectly possible that O'Brien had really

234
00:14:47,977 --> 00:14:49,599
forgotten the photograph.

235
00:14:49,599 --> 00:14:53,137
And if so, then already he would have forgotten his denial of remembering it,

236
00:14:53,137 --> 00:14:55,540
and forgotten the act of forgetting.

237
00:14:55,540 --> 00:14:59,439
How could one be sure that it was simple trickery? Perhaps that lunatic

238
00:14:59,439 --> 00:15:05,378
dislocation in the mind could really happen: that was the thoughts that defeated him.

239
00:15:05,378 --> 00:15:09,279
O'Brien was looking down at him speculatively. More than ever he had the

240
00:15:09,279 --> 00:15:13,297
air of a teacher taking pains with a wayward but promising child.

241
00:15:13,297 --> 00:15:17,558
'There is a Party slogan dealing with the control of the past,' he said. 'Repeat it, if

242
00:15:17,558 --> 00:15:19,297
you please.'

243
00:15:19,297 --> 00:15:23,319
'"Who controls the past controls the future: who controls the present controls

244
00:15:23,319 --> 00:15:28,360
the past,"' repeated Winston obediently. '"Who controls the present controls the past,"'

245
00:15:28,360 --> 00:15:31,599
said O'Brien, nodding his head with slow approval.

246
00:15:31,599 --> 00:15:36,099
'Is it your opinion, Winston, that the past has real existence?'

247
00:15:36,099 --> 00:15:41,619
Again the feeling of helplessness descended upon Winston. His eyes splitted towards the dial.

248
00:15:41,619 --> 00:15:45,099
He not only did not know whether 'yes' or 'no' was the answer that would save him

249
00:15:45,099 --> 00:15:50,500
from pain; he did not even know which answer he believed to be the true one.

250
00:15:50,500 --> 00:15:53,200
O'Brien smiled faintly.'

251
00:15:53,200 --> 00:15:56,259
.'You are no metaphysician, Winston,' he said.

252
00:15:56,259 --> 00:16:00,340
'Until this moment you had never considered what is meant by existence.

253
00:16:00,340 --> 00:16:02,439
I will put it more precisely.

254
00:16:02,439 --> 00:16:05,740
Does the past exist concretely, in space?

255
00:16:05,740 --> 00:16:09,637
Is there somewhere or rather a place, a world of solid objects, where the past is

256
00:16:09,637 --> 00:16:11,797
still happening?'

257
00:16:11,797 --> 00:16:13,659
'No.'

258
00:16:13,659 --> 00:16:16,718
'Then where does the past exist, if at all?'

259
00:16:16,718 --> 00:16:19,599
'In records. It is written down.'

260
00:16:19,599 --> 00:16:25,900
'In records. And— 'In the mind. In human memories.'

261
00:16:25,900 --> 00:16:31,119
'In memory. Very well, then. We, the Party, control all records, and we control all

262
00:16:31,119 --> 00:16:35,200
memories. Then we control the past, do we not?'

263
00:16:35,200 --> 00:16:38,919
'But how can you stop people remembering things?' cried Winston, again momentarily

264
00:16:38,919 --> 00:16:41,020
for getting the dial. 'It is involuntary.

265
00:16:41,020 --> 00:16:46,477
It is outside oneself. How can you control memory? You have not controlled mine!'

266
00:16:46,477 --> 00:16:51,400
O'Brien's Manor grew stern again. He laid his hand on the dial.

267
00:16:51,400 --> 00:16:56,378
'On the contrary,' he said, 'you have not controlled it. That is what has brought you here

268
00:16:56,378 --> 00:17:00,878
because you have failed in humility, in self-discipline. You would not make the

269
00:17:00,878 --> 00:17:05,378
act of submission which is the price of sanity. You prefer to be a lunatic, a

270
00:17:05,378 --> 00:17:07,000
minority of one.

271
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:10,599
Only the disciplined mind can see reality, Winston.

272
00:17:10,599 --> 00:17:15,340
You believe that reality is something objective, external, existing in its own right.

273
00:17:15,340 --> 00:17:18,637
You also believe that the nature of reality is self-evident

274
00:17:18,637 --> 00:17:22,240
When you delude yourself into thinking that you see something, you assume that

275
00:17:22,240 --> 00:17:24,819
everyone else sees the same thing as you.

276
00:17:24,819 --> 00:17:29,319
But I tell you, Winston, that reality is not external. Reality exists in the human

277
00:17:29,319 --> 00:17:31,119
mind, and nowhere else.

278
00:17:31,119 --> 00:17:36,279
Not in the individual mind, which can make mistakes, and in any case soon perishes:

279
00:17:36,279 --> 00:17:40,058
only in the mind of the Party, which is collective and immortal.

280
00:17:40,058 --> 00:17:45,457
Whatever the Party holds to be the truth, is truth. It is impossible to see reality

281
00:17:45,457 --> 00:17:48,099
accepted by looking through the eyes of the Party.

282
00:17:48,099 --> 00:17:51,340
That is the fact that you have got to relearn Winston.

283
00:17:51,340 --> 00:17:55,180
It needs an act of self-destruction and effort of the will. You must humble

284
00:17:55,180 --> 00:17:58,058
yourself before you can become sane.'

285
00:17:58,058 --> 00:18:03,759
He paused for a few moments, as though to allow what he had been saying to sink in.

286
00:18:03,759 --> 00:18:07,718
'Do you remember,' he went on, 'writing in your diary, "Freedom is the freedom to say

287
00:18:07,718 --> 00:18:10,058
that two plus two make four"?'

288
00:18:10,058 --> 00:18:12,297
'Yes,' said Winston.

289
00:18:12,297 --> 00:18:16,779
O'Brien held up his left hand it's back towards Winston, with the thumb hidden

290
00:18:16,779 --> 00:18:19,240
and the four fingers extended.

291
00:18:19,240 --> 00:18:22,180
'How many fingers am I holding up, Winston?'

292
00:18:22,180 --> 00:18:23,919
'Four.'

293
00:18:23,919 --> 00:18:28,477
'And if the Party says that it is not four but five— —then how many?'

294
00:18:28,477 --> 00:18:29,680
'Four.'

295
00:18:29,680 --> 00:18:32,378
The word ended in a gasp of pain.

296
00:18:32,378 --> 00:18:36,637
The needle of the dial had shot up to 55. The sweat had sprung out all over

297
00:18:36,637 --> 00:18:40,718
Winston's body. The air tore into his lungs and issued again in deep groans

298
00:18:40,718 --> 00:18:44,020
which even by clenching his teeth he could not stop.

299
00:18:44,020 --> 00:18:48,040
O'Brien watched him, the four fingers still extended. He drew back the lever.

300
00:18:48,040 --> 00:18:51,580
This time the pain was only slightly eased.

301
00:18:51,580 --> 00:18:54,759
'How many fingers, Winston?' 'Four.'

302
00:18:54,759 --> 00:18:59,439
The needle went up to 60. How many fingers, Winston?'

303
00:18:59,439 --> 00:19:02,860
'Four! Four! What else can I say? Four!'

304
00:19:02,860 --> 00:19:07,000
The needle must have risen again, but he did not look at it. The heavy Stone face

305
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:09,159
and the four fingers filled his vision.

306
00:19:09,159 --> 00:19:13,058
The fingers stood up before his eyes like pillars, enormous, blurry and seeming

307
00:19:13,058 --> 00:19:18,520
to vibrate, but unmistakably for How many fingers, Winston?'

308
00:19:18,520 --> 00:19:21,159
'Four! Stop it, stop it! How can you go on?

309
00:19:21,159 --> 00:19:22,659
Four! Four!'

310
00:19:22,659 --> 00:19:24,878
'How many fingers, Winston?'

311
00:19:24,878 --> 00:19:28,240
'Five! Five! Five!'

312
00:19:28,240 --> 00:19:30,580
'No, Winston, that is no use. You are lying.

313
00:19:30,580 --> 00:19:34,240
You still think there are four. How many fingers, please?'

314
00:19:34,240 --> 00:19:40,360
'Four! Five! Four! Anything you like. Only stop it, stop the pain!'

315
00:19:40,360 --> 00:19:43,957
Abruptly he was sitting up with her Brand's arm around his shoulders.

316
00:19:43,957 --> 00:19:47,137
He had perhaps lost consciousness for a few seconds.

317
00:19:47,137 --> 00:19:51,400
The bonds that had held his body down were loosened. He felt very cold, he was

318
00:19:51,400 --> 00:19:55,900
shaking uncontrollably, his teeth were chattering, the tears were rolling down his cheeks.

319
00:19:55,900 --> 00:19:59,919
For a moment he clung to a brine like a baby, curiously comforted by the heavy

320
00:19:59,919 --> 00:20:01,718
arm around his shoulders.

321
00:20:01,718 --> 00:20:05,137
He had the feeling that O'Brien was his protector, that the pain was something

322
00:20:05,137 --> 00:20:09,340
that came from outside, from some other source, and that it was O'Brien who would

323
00:20:09,340 --> 00:20:11,919
save him from it.

324
00:20:11,919 --> 00:20:15,099
'You are a slow learner, Winston,' said O'Brien gently.

325
00:20:15,099 --> 00:20:17,619
'How can I help it?' he blubbard How can I

326
00:20:17,619 --> 00:20:19,360
help seeing what is in front of my eyes?

327
00:20:19,360 --> 00:20:22,058
Two and two are four.'

328
00:20:22,058 --> 00:20:23,860
'Sometimes, Winston.

329
00:20:23,860 --> 00:20:28,297
Sometimes they are five. Sometimes they're three. Sometimes they're all of

330
00:20:28,297 --> 00:20:33,637
them at once. You must try harder. It is not easy to become sane.'

331
00:20:33,637 --> 00:20:37,779
He laid Winston down on the bed. The grip of his limbs tightened again, but the

332
00:20:37,779 --> 00:20:42,400
pain had ebbed away and the trembling had stopped, leaving him merely weak and cold.

333
00:20:42,400 --> 00:20:46,360
O'Brien motioned with his head to the man in the white coat, who had stored

334
00:20:46,360 --> 00:20:48,637
immobile throughout the proceedings.

335
00:20:48,637 --> 00:20:53,080
The man in the white coat bent down and looked closely into Winston's eyes, felt

336
00:20:53,080 --> 00:20:59,020
his pulse, laid an ear against his chest, tapped here and there; then he nodded through Orion

337
00:20:59,020 --> 00:21:03,759
Again,' said O'Brien The pain flowed into Winston's body. The

338
00:21:03,759 --> 00:21:08,860
needle must be at 70 75 He had shut his eyes this time. He knew that the fingers

339
00:21:08,860 --> 00:21:12,700
were still there, and still four. All that mattered was somehow to stay alive until

340
00:21:12,700 --> 00:21:14,378
the spasm was over.

341
00:21:14,378 --> 00:21:17,680
He had ceased to notice whether he was crying out or not.

342
00:21:17,680 --> 00:21:21,040
The pain lessened again. He opened his eyes.

343
00:21:21,040 --> 00:21:24,279
O'Brien had drawn back the lever.

344
00:21:24,279 --> 00:21:26,500
'How many fingers, Winston?'

345
00:21:26,500 --> 00:21:32,619
'Four. I suppose there are four. I would see five if I could I'm trying to see five.'

346
00:21:32,619 --> 00:21:37,119
'Which do you wish: to persuade me that you see five, or really to see them?'

347
00:21:37,119 --> 00:21:39,099
'Really to see them.'

348
00:21:39,099 --> 00:21:41,200
'Again,' said O'Brien.

349
00:21:41,200 --> 00:21:45,700
Perhaps the needle was 80 90. weinstead could not intermittently remember why

350
00:21:45,700 --> 00:21:49,718
the pain was happening. Behind his screwed up eyelids a Forester fingers

351
00:21:49,718 --> 00:21:53,860
seemed to be moving in a sort of dance, weaving in and out, disappearing behind

352
00:21:53,860 --> 00:21:56,200
one another and reappearing again.

353
00:21:56,200 --> 00:22:00,218
He was trying to counter them, he could not remember why. He knew only that it

354
00:22:00,218 --> 00:22:03,457
was impossible to count them, and that this was somehow due to the mysterious

355
00:22:03,457 --> 00:22:05,860
identity between five and four.

356
00:22:05,860 --> 00:22:07,900
The pain died down again.

357
00:22:07,900 --> 00:22:11,580
When he opened his eyes it was to find that he was still seeing the same thing.

358
00:22:11,580 --> 00:22:15,279
Innumerable fingers, like moving trees, were still streaming past in either

359
00:22:15,279 --> 00:22:20,020
direction, crossing and re-crossing He shut his eyes again.

360
00:22:20,020 --> 00:22:22,779
'How many fingers am I holding up, Winston?'

361
00:22:22,779 --> 00:22:24,159
'I don't know.

362
00:22:24,159 --> 00:22:29,378
I don't know. You will kill me if you do that again. Four, five, six— —in all honesty

363
00:22:29,378 --> 00:22:30,878
I don't know.'

364
00:22:30,878 --> 00:22:33,520
'Better,' said O'Brien.

365
00:22:33,520 --> 00:22:38,137
A needle slid into Winston's arm. Almost in the same instant a blissful, healing

366
00:22:38,137 --> 00:22:40,058
warmth spread all through his body.

367
00:22:40,058 --> 00:22:43,900
The pain was already half forgotten He opened his eyes and looked up

368
00:22:43,900 --> 00:22:48,580
gratefully at O'Brien. At the site of the heavy, lined face, so ugly and so

369
00:22:48,580 --> 00:22:51,939
intelligent, his heart seemed to turn over.

370
00:22:51,939 --> 00:22:56,200
If he could have moved he would have stretched out a hand and laid it on O'Brien's arm.

371
00:22:56,200 --> 00:23:01,058
He had never loved him so deeply as at this moment, and not merely because he had stopped

372
00:23:01,058 --> 00:23:05,319
the pain. The old feeling, that at bottom it did not matter whether O'Brien was a

373
00:23:05,319 --> 00:23:07,659
friend or an enemy, had come back.

374
00:23:07,659 --> 00:23:12,099
O'Brien was a person who could be talked to. Perhaps one did not want to be loved

375
00:23:12,099 --> 00:23:16,360
so much as to be understood. O'Brien had tortured him to the edge of lunacy, and

376
00:23:16,360 --> 00:23:19,360
in a little while, it was certain, he would send him to his death.

377
00:23:19,360 --> 00:23:24,580
It made no difference. In some sense that went deeper than friendship, they were intimates:

378
00:23:24,580 --> 00:23:28,779
somewhere or other, although the actual words might never be spoken, there was a

379
00:23:28,779 --> 00:23:30,637
place where they could meet and talk.

380
00:23:30,637 --> 00:23:34,297
O'Brien was looking down at him with an expression which suggested that the same

381
00:23:34,297 --> 00:23:36,340
thought might be in his own mind.

382
00:23:36,340 --> 00:23:40,659
When he spoke it was in an easy, conversational tone.

383
00:23:40,659 --> 00:23:43,240
'Do you know where you are, Winston?' he said.

384
00:23:43,240 --> 00:23:47,740
'I don't know. I can guess. In the Ministry of Love.'

385
00:23:47,740 --> 00:23:50,378
'Do you know how long you have been here?'

386
00:23:50,378 --> 00:23:55,297
'I don't know. Days, weeks, months— —I think it is months.'

387
00:23:55,297 --> 00:23:59,080
'And why do you imagine that we bring people to this place?'

388
00:23:59,080 --> 00:24:01,058
'To make them confess.'

389
00:24:01,058 --> 00:24:04,180
'No, that is not the reason. Try again.'

390
00:24:04,180 --> 00:24:05,558
'To punish them.'

391
00:24:05,558 --> 00:24:10,477
'No!' exclaimed a bran His voice had changed extraordinarily, and his face had

392
00:24:10,477 --> 00:24:13,419
suddenly become both stern and animated.

393
00:24:13,419 --> 00:24:17,378
'No! Not merely to extract your confession not to punish you.

394
00:24:17,378 --> 00:24:22,599
Shall I tell you why we have brought you here? To cure you! To make you sane! Will

395
00:24:22,599 --> 00:24:25,779
you understand, Winston, that no one whom we bring to this place ever leaves our

396
00:24:25,779 --> 00:24:30,279
hands uncured? We are not interested in those stupid crimes that you have committed.

397
00:24:30,279 --> 00:24:35,319
The Party is not interested in the overt act: the thought is all we care about.

398
00:24:35,319 --> 00:24:38,619
We do not merely destroy our enemies, we change them.

399
00:24:38,619 --> 00:24:41,619
Do you understand what I mean by that?'

400
00:24:41,619 --> 00:24:45,279
He was bending over Winston. His face looked enormous because of its nearness,

401
00:24:45,279 --> 00:24:48,159
and hideously ugly because it was seen from below.

402
00:24:48,159 --> 00:24:52,659
Moreover it was filled with a sort of exaltation, a lunatic intensity.

403
00:24:52,659 --> 00:24:56,977
Again Winston's heart shrank. If it had been possible he would have cowered

404
00:24:56,977 --> 00:25:00,878
deeper into the bed. He felt certain that O'Brien was about to twist the dial out

405
00:25:00,878 --> 00:25:04,957
of sheer wantedness At this moment, however, O'Brien turned

406
00:25:04,957 --> 00:25:07,119
away. He took a pace or two up and down.

407
00:25:07,119 --> 00:25:12,457
Then he continued less vehemently: 'The first thing for you to understand is

408
00:25:12,457 --> 00:25:16,419
that in this place there are no martyrdoms. You have rid of the religious

409
00:25:16,419 --> 00:25:20,919
persecutions of the past. In the Middle Ages there was the Inquisition. It was a

410
00:25:20,919 --> 00:25:26,020
failure. It set out to eradicate heresy, and ended by perpetuating it. For every

411
00:25:26,020 --> 00:25:30,520
heretic it burnt at the stake, thousands of others rose up. Why was that?

412
00:25:30,520 --> 00:25:33,939
Because the Inquisition killed its enemies in the open, and killed them

413
00:25:33,939 --> 00:25:38,680
while they were still unrepented in fact, it killed them because they were unrepentant.

414
00:25:38,680 --> 00:25:42,759
Men were dying because they would not abandon their true beliefs. Naturally all

415
00:25:42,759 --> 00:25:46,840
the glory belonged to the victim and all the shame to the Inquisitor who burnt him.

416
00:25:46,840 --> 00:25:50,919
Later, in the 20th century, there were totalitarians, as they were called.

417
00:25:50,919 --> 00:25:54,637
There were the German Nazis and the Russian Communists. The Russians

418
00:25:54,637 --> 00:25:57,878
persecuted heresy more cruelly than the Inquisition had done.

419
00:25:57,878 --> 00:26:01,419
And they imagined that they had learned from the mistakes of the past;

420
00:26:01,419 --> 00:26:06,580
they knew, at any rate, that one must not make matters Before they exposed their victims to

421
00:26:06,580 --> 00:26:10,297
public trial, they deliberately set themselves to destroy their dignity. They

422
00:26:10,297 --> 00:26:14,619
wore them down by torture and solitude until they were despicable, cringing riches

423
00:26:14,619 --> 00:26:18,520
confessing whatever was put into their mouths, covering themselves with abuse,

424
00:26:18,520 --> 00:26:22,718
accusing and sheltering behind one another, whimpering for mercy.

425
00:26:22,718 --> 00:26:26,740
And yet after only a few years the same thing had happened ever again. The dead

426
00:26:26,740 --> 00:26:30,939
men had to become martyrs and their degradation was forgotten. Once again, why

427
00:26:30,939 --> 00:26:34,659
was it? In the first place, because the confessions that they had made were

428
00:26:34,659 --> 00:26:38,977
obviously extorted and untrue. We do not make mistakes of that kind.

429
00:26:38,977 --> 00:26:41,797
All the confessions that are added here are true.

430
00:26:41,797 --> 00:26:46,599
We make them true. And above all we do not allow the dead to rise up against us.

431
00:26:46,599 --> 00:26:50,797
You must stop imagining that posterity will vindicate you, Winston.

432
00:26:50,797 --> 00:26:54,700
Posterity will never hear of you. You will be lifted clean out from the stream

433
00:26:54,700 --> 00:26:58,900
of history. We shall turn you into gas and pour you into the stratosphere.

434
00:26:58,900 --> 00:27:02,680
Nothing will remain of you; not a name in a register, not a memory in a living

435
00:27:02,680 --> 00:27:07,000
brain. You will be annihilated in the past as well as in the future. You will

436
00:27:07,000 --> 00:27:13,180
never have existed and why bother to torture me? thought Winston, with a momentary bitterness.

437
00:27:13,180 --> 00:27:16,900
O'Brien checked his step as though Winston had uttered the thought aloud.

438
00:27:16,900 --> 00:27:21,340
His large ugly face came nearer, with the eyes a little narrowed.

439
00:27:21,340 --> 00:27:25,477
'You were thinking,' he said, 'that since we intend to destroy you utterly, so that

440
00:27:25,477 --> 00:27:28,718
nothing that you say or do can make the smallest difference—

441
00:27:28,718 --> 00:27:32,378
—in that case, why do we go to the trouble of interrogating you first?

442
00:27:32,378 --> 00:27:35,137
That is what you were thinking, was it not?'

443
00:27:35,137 --> 00:27:37,957
'Yes,' said Winston.

444
00:27:37,957 --> 00:27:40,240
O'Brien smiled slightly.

445
00:27:40,240 --> 00:27:43,297
'You were a flaw in the pattern, Winston.

446
00:27:43,297 --> 00:27:45,279
You were a stain that must be wiped out.

447
00:27:45,279 --> 00:27:49,718
Did I not tell you just now that we are different from the persecutors of the past?

448
00:27:49,718 --> 00:27:54,878
We are not content with negative obedience, nor even with the most abject submission.

449
00:27:54,878 --> 00:27:58,718
When finally you surrender to us, it must be of your own free will.

450
00:27:58,718 --> 00:28:03,040
We do not destroy the heretic because he resists us: so long as he resists us we

451
00:28:03,040 --> 00:28:08,137
never destroy him. We convert him, we capture his inner mind, we reshape him. We

452
00:28:08,137 --> 00:28:12,159
burn all evil and all the illusion out of him; we bring him over to our side, not

453
00:28:12,159 --> 00:28:14,740
in appearance, but genuinely, heart and soul.

454
00:28:14,740 --> 00:28:19,360
We make him one of ourselves before we kill him. It is intolerable to us that an

455
00:28:19,360 --> 00:28:22,540
erroneous thought should exist anywhere in the world, however secret and

456
00:28:22,540 --> 00:28:27,819
powerless it may be. Even in the instant of death we cannot permit any deviation.

457
00:28:27,819 --> 00:28:31,718
In the old days the heretic walked to the stakes still a heretic, proclaiming

458
00:28:31,718 --> 00:28:33,939
his heresy exalting in it.

459
00:28:33,939 --> 00:28:37,477
Even the victim of the Russian purges could carry rebellion locked up in his

460
00:28:37,477 --> 00:28:40,840
skull as he walked down the passage waiting for the bullet.

461
00:28:40,840 --> 00:28:45,099
But we make the brain perfect before we blow it out. The command of the old

462
00:28:45,099 --> 00:28:47,619
despotisms was "Thou shalt not".

463
00:28:47,619 --> 00:28:51,700
The command of the totalitarians was "Thou shalt".

464
00:28:51,700 --> 00:28:58,000
Our command is Thou art No one whom we bring to this place ever stands out against us.

465
00:28:58,000 --> 00:28:59,977
Everyone is washed clean.

466
00:28:59,977 --> 00:29:04,240
Even those three miserable traitors in whose innocence you once believed— —Jones,

467
00:29:04,240 --> 00:29:08,619
Aaronson and Rutherford— —in the end we broke them down. I took part in their

468
00:29:08,619 --> 00:29:13,957
interrogation myself. I saw them gradually worn down, whimpering groveling weeping—

469
00:29:13,957 --> 00:29:18,637
—and in the end it was not with pain or fear, only with penitence.

470
00:29:18,637 --> 00:29:22,419
By the time we had finished with them there were only the shells of men.

471
00:29:22,419 --> 00:29:27,637
There was nothing left in them except sorrow for what they had done, and love of Big Brother.

472
00:29:27,637 --> 00:29:31,779
It was touching to see how they loved him. They begged to be shot quickly, so

473
00:29:31,779 --> 00:29:35,319
that they could die while their minds were still clean.'

474
00:29:35,319 --> 00:29:37,718
His voice had grown almost dreamy.

475
00:29:37,718 --> 00:29:41,977
The exaltation, the lunatic enthusiasm, was still in his face.

476
00:29:41,977 --> 00:29:46,659
He is not pretending, thought Winston; he is not a hypocrite; he believes every word he says.

477
00:29:46,659 --> 00:29:51,279
What most oppressed him was the consciousness of his own intellectual imperiority

478
00:29:51,279 --> 00:29:55,779
He watched the heavy yet graceful formed strolling to and fro, in and out of the

479
00:29:55,779 --> 00:29:57,279
range of his vision.

480
00:29:57,279 --> 00:30:00,520
O'Brien was a being in always larger than himself.

481
00:30:00,520 --> 00:30:04,000
There was no idea that he had ever had, or could have, that O'Brien had not long

482
00:30:04,000 --> 00:30:06,457
ago known, examined and rejected.

483
00:30:06,457 --> 00:30:09,218
His mind contained Winston's mind.

484
00:30:09,218 --> 00:30:12,637
But in that case how could it be true that O'Brien was mad?

485
00:30:12,637 --> 00:30:15,819
It must be he, Winston, who was mad.

486
00:30:15,819 --> 00:30:17,860
O'Brien haltered and looked down at him.

487
00:30:17,860 --> 00:30:22,718
His voice had grown Stone again foreign Do not imagine that you will save

488
00:30:22,718 --> 00:30:25,840
yourself, Winston, however completely you surrender to us.

489
00:30:25,840 --> 00:30:30,637
No one who has once gone astray has ever spared. And even if we chose to let you

490
00:30:30,637 --> 00:30:34,840
live out the natural term of your life, still you would never escape from us.

491
00:30:34,840 --> 00:30:39,878
What happens to you here is forever Understand that in advance.

492
00:30:39,878 --> 00:30:44,378
We shall crush you down to the point from which there is no coming back.

493
00:30:44,378 --> 00:30:48,939
Things will happen to you from which you could not recover, if you lived a thousand years.

494
00:30:48,939 --> 00:30:52,840
Never again will you be capable of ordinary human feeling. Everything will

495
00:30:52,840 --> 00:30:57,580
be dead inside you. Never again will you be capable of love, or friendship, or joy

496
00:30:57,580 --> 00:31:02,919
of living, or laughter, or curiosity, or courage, or integrity. You will be hollow.

497
00:31:02,919 --> 00:31:07,860
We shall squeeze you empty, and then we shall fill you with ourselves.'

498
00:31:07,860 --> 00:31:11,439
He paused and signed to the man in the white coat.

499
00:31:11,439 --> 00:31:16,659
Winston was aware of some heavy piece of apparatus being pushed into place behind his head.

500
00:31:16,659 --> 00:31:23,137
O'Brien had sat down beside the bed, so that his face was almost on a level with Winston's.

501
00:31:23,137 --> 00:31:28,000
'Three thousand,' he said, speaking of a Winston's head to the man in the white coat.

502
00:31:28,000 --> 00:31:33,340
Two soft pads, which felt slightly moist, clamped themselves against Winston's temples.

503
00:31:33,340 --> 00:31:36,939
He quailed. There was pain coming, a new kind of pain.

504
00:31:36,939 --> 00:31:41,860
O'Brien laid a hand reassuringly, almost kindly, on his.

505
00:31:41,860 --> 00:31:46,540
'This time it will not hurt,' he said. 'Keep your eyes fixed on mine.'

506
00:31:46,540 --> 00:31:49,840
At this moment there was a devastating explosion of what seemed like an

507
00:31:49,840 --> 00:31:53,319
explosion, though it was not certain whether there was any noise. There was

508
00:31:53,319 --> 00:31:54,957
undoubtedly a blinding flash of light.

509
00:31:54,957 --> 00:31:57,878
Winston was not hurt, only prostrated.

510
00:31:57,878 --> 00:32:01,599
Although he had already been lying on his back when the thing happened, he had

511
00:32:01,599 --> 00:32:04,599
a curious feeling that he had been knocked into that position.

512
00:32:04,599 --> 00:32:09,939
A terrific, painless blow had flattened him out. Also something had happened inside his head.

513
00:32:09,939 --> 00:32:13,540
As his eyes regained their focus he remembered who he was, and where he was,

514
00:32:13,540 --> 00:32:17,619
and recognized the face that was gazing into his own; but somewhere or rather

515
00:32:17,619 --> 00:32:22,297
there was a large patch of emptiness, as though a piece had been taken out of his brain.

516
00:32:22,297 --> 00:32:28,000
'It will not last,' said O'Brien looked me in the eyes. What country is Oceania at war with?'

517
00:32:28,000 --> 00:32:29,558
Winston thought.

518
00:32:29,558 --> 00:32:34,360
He knew what was meant by Oceania, and that he himself was a citizen of Oceania.

519
00:32:34,360 --> 00:32:38,919
He also remembered Eurasia and East Asia but who was at war with whom he did not

520
00:32:38,919 --> 00:32:42,520
know. In fact he had not been aware that there was any war.

521
00:32:42,520 --> 00:32:45,279
'I don't remember.'

522
00:32:45,279 --> 00:32:49,297
'Oceania is at war with East Asia Do you remember that now?'

523
00:32:49,297 --> 00:32:50,797
'Yes.'

524
00:32:50,797 --> 00:32:54,759
Oceania has always been at war with East Asia Since the beginning of your life,

525
00:32:54,759 --> 00:32:58,718
since the beginning of the Party, since the beginning of history, the war has

526
00:32:58,718 --> 00:33:03,599
continued without a break, always the same war. Do you remember that?'

527
00:33:03,599 --> 00:33:09,759
'Yes 11 years ago you created a legend about three men who had been condemned to

528
00:33:09,759 --> 00:33:13,360
death for treachery. You pretended that you had seen a piece of paper which

529
00:33:13,360 --> 00:33:14,860
proved them innocent.

530
00:33:14,860 --> 00:33:20,319
No such piece of paper ever existed. You invented it, and later you grew to believe in it.

531
00:33:20,319 --> 00:33:25,240
You remember now the very moment at which you first invented it. Do you remember that?'

532
00:33:25,240 --> 00:33:27,040
'Yes.'

533
00:33:27,040 --> 00:33:31,718
'Just now I held up the fingers of my hand to you. You saw five fingers. Do you

534
00:33:31,718 --> 00:33:32,797
remember that?'

535
00:33:32,797 --> 00:33:38,558
'Yes where Brian held up the fingers of his left hand, with the thumb concealed.

536
00:33:38,558 --> 00:33:42,457
'There are five fingers there. Do you see five fingers?'

537
00:33:42,457 --> 00:33:43,898
'Yes.'

538
00:33:43,898 --> 00:33:47,259
And he did see them, for a fleeting instant, before the scenery of his mind

539
00:33:47,259 --> 00:33:51,819
changed. He saw five fingers, and there was no deformity.

540
00:33:51,819 --> 00:33:56,080
Then everything was normal again, and the old fear, the hatred and the bewilderment

541
00:33:56,080 --> 00:33:57,878
came crowding back again.

542
00:33:57,878 --> 00:34:02,500
But there had been a moment— —he did not know how long 30 seconds, perhaps— —of

543
00:34:02,500 --> 00:34:08,080
luminous certainty, when each new suggestion of her Brands had built up a patch of emptiness and

544
00:34:08,080 --> 00:34:12,340
become absolute truth, and when two and two could have been three as easily as

545
00:34:12,340 --> 00:34:14,977
five, if that were what was needed.

546
00:34:14,977 --> 00:34:19,000
It had faded but before O'Brien had dropped his hand; but though he could not

547
00:34:19,000 --> 00:34:23,080
recapture it, he could remember it, as one remembers a vivid experience at some

548
00:34:23,080 --> 00:34:27,637
period of one's life when one was in effect a different person.

549
00:34:27,637 --> 00:34:31,599
'You see now,' said O'Brien, 'that it is at any rate possible.'

550
00:34:31,599 --> 00:34:34,297
'Yes,' said Winston.

551
00:34:34,297 --> 00:34:39,159
O'Brien stood up with a satisfied heir Over to his left Winston saw the man in

552
00:34:39,159 --> 00:34:43,058
the white coat break an ample and draw back the plunger of a syringe.

553
00:34:43,058 --> 00:34:45,218
O'Brien turned to Winston with a smile.

554
00:34:45,218 --> 00:34:49,297
In almost the old manner he resettled his spectacles on his nose.

555
00:34:49,297 --> 00:34:53,137
'Do you remember writing in your diary,' he said, 'that it did not matter whether I

556
00:34:53,137 --> 00:34:56,619
was a friend or an enemy, since I was at least a person who understood you and

557
00:34:56,619 --> 00:34:58,297
could be talked to?

558
00:34:58,297 --> 00:35:00,878
You were right. I enjoyed talking to you.

559
00:35:00,878 --> 00:35:06,700
Your mind appeals to me. It resembles my own mind except that you happen to be insane.

560
00:35:06,700 --> 00:35:11,439
Before we bring the session to an end you can ask me a few questions, if you choose.'

561
00:35:11,439 --> 00:35:13,360
'Any question I like?'

562
00:35:13,360 --> 00:35:14,558
'Anything.'

563
00:35:14,558 --> 00:35:18,700
He saw that Winston's eyes were upon the dial. 'It is switched off.

564
00:35:18,700 --> 00:35:21,279
What is your first question?'

565
00:35:21,279 --> 00:35:24,218
'What have you done with Julia?' said Winston.

566
00:35:24,218 --> 00:35:26,500
O'Brien smiled again.

567
00:35:26,500 --> 00:35:31,718
'She betrayed you, Winston. Immediately— —unreservedly. I have seldom seen anyone

568
00:35:31,718 --> 00:35:33,878
come over to us so promptly.

569
00:35:33,878 --> 00:35:37,477
You would hardly recognize her if you saw her. All her rebelliousness, her

570
00:35:37,477 --> 00:35:42,520
deceit, her folly, her dirty-mindedness— —everything has been burned out of her. It

571
00:35:42,520 --> 00:35:46,000
was a perfect conversion, a textbook case.'

572
00:35:46,000 --> 00:35:47,740
'You tortured her?'

573
00:35:47,740 --> 00:35:51,939
O'Brien left this unanswered. 'Next question,' he said.

574
00:35:51,939 --> 00:35:54,700
'Does Big Brother exist?'

575
00:35:54,700 --> 00:36:01,240
'Of course he exists. The Party exists. Big Brother is the embodiment of the Party.'

576
00:36:01,240 --> 00:36:04,540
'Does he exist in the same way as I exist?'

577
00:36:04,540 --> 00:36:07,119
'You do not exist,' said O'Brien.

578
00:36:07,119 --> 00:36:10,119
Once again the sense of helplessness assailed him.

579
00:36:10,119 --> 00:36:12,939
He knew, or he could imagine, the arguments which proved his own

580
00:36:12,939 --> 00:36:17,319
non-existence but they were nonsense, they were only a play on words.

581
00:36:17,319 --> 00:36:21,637
Did not the statement, 'You do not exist', contain a logical absurdity?

582
00:36:21,637 --> 00:36:25,180
But what use was it to say so? His mind shriveled as he thought of the

583
00:36:25,180 --> 00:36:30,119
unanswerable, mad arguments with which O'Brien would demolish him.

584
00:36:30,159 --> 00:36:35,558
'I think I exist,' he said wearily. 'I am conscious of my own identity. I was born,

585
00:36:35,558 --> 00:36:41,080
and I shall die. I have arms and legs. I occupy a particular point in space. No

586
00:36:41,080 --> 00:36:44,740
other solid object can occupy the same point simultaneously.

587
00:36:44,740 --> 00:36:47,740
In that sense, does Big Brother exist?'

588
00:36:47,740 --> 00:36:50,977
'It is of no importance. He exists.'

589
00:36:50,977 --> 00:36:53,080
'Will Big Brother ever die?'

590
00:36:53,080 --> 00:36:57,218
'Of course not. How could he die? Next question.'

591
00:36:57,218 --> 00:36:59,340
'Does the Brotherhood exist?'

592
00:36:59,340 --> 00:37:03,580
'That, Winston, you will never know. If we choose to set you free when we are

593
00:37:03,580 --> 00:37:07,477
finished with you, and if you live to be 90 years old, still you will never learn

594
00:37:07,477 --> 00:37:11,439
whether the answer to that question is Yes or No. As long as you leave it will

595
00:37:11,439 --> 00:37:14,860
be an unsolved riddle in your mind.'

596
00:37:14,860 --> 00:37:19,240
Winston lay silent. His breast rose and fell a little faster.

597
00:37:19,240 --> 00:37:23,977
He still had not asked the question that had come into his mind the first. He had

598
00:37:23,977 --> 00:37:27,759
got to ask it, and yet it was as though his tongue would not utter it.

599
00:37:27,759 --> 00:37:30,637
There was a trace of amusement in O'Brien's face.

600
00:37:30,637 --> 00:37:34,297
Even his spectacles seemed to wear an ironical gleam.

601
00:37:34,297 --> 00:37:38,619
He knows, thought Winston suddenly, he knows what I am going to ask! At the

602
00:37:38,619 --> 00:37:44,020
thoughts the words burst out of him: 'What is in Room 101?'

603
00:37:44,020 --> 00:37:48,457
The expression on O'Brien's face did not change. He answered dryly

604
00:37:48,457 --> 00:37:50,500
You know what is in Room 101, Winston.

605
00:37:50,500 --> 00:37:55,718
Everyone knows what is in Room 101.'. He raised a finger to the man in the white

606
00:37:55,718 --> 00:37:58,599
coat. Evidently the session was at an end.

607
00:37:58,599 --> 00:38:04,000
A needle jerked into Winston's arm. He sank almost instantly into deep sleep
